<?xml version='1.0' ?>
<rrx namespace="Email.MSGraph" v="9.1.9.2">
	<com ref="Datacap.Libraries.Email.MSGraph.Actions"  qi="Email.MSGraph action library">
		<h>
			The Email action library using the Microsoft Graph API.<br/><br/>

      This action library allows creation of an email at runtime, with optional attachments.
      The emails will be sent using a Microsoft Office 365 account.
      The email recipient, subject and body can be set at runtime.
      If attachments are added to the email, they have total size limit of 3 MB.<br/><br/>
      
      The actions in this action library must be used together and cannot be mixed with other
      email action libraries.<br/><br/>
		</h>

		<method name="ms_login" qi="Login to Office 365 using the Microsoft Graph API.">
			<p name="clientId" type="string" />
			<p name="tenantId" type="string" />
			<p name="username" type="string" />
			<p name="password" type="string" />
			<ap>
				clientId : Application (client) ID from Azure Active Directory - App registrations.<br/>
				tenantId : Directory (tenant) ID from Azure Active Directory - App registrations.<br/>
				username : Username of Office 365 account.<br/>
				password : Password of Office 365 account.<br/><br/>

				Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>

				The parameters (clientId, tenantId, username, password) can be encrypted by storing them in the Advanced section of the Custom tab in the Application Manager.<br/><br/>
        
				To retrieve parameters that is stored in the Advanced section,
				use a smart parameter for the action's parameters similar to
				@APPVAR(values/adv/password) where password is the name entered for the value in the Application Manager.
			</ap>
			<h>
        This action will login to the Office 365 mail service, allowing emails to be sent from Datacap at runtime. 
        Once logged in, the other actions in this email library can be used to create an email to send.
        The action uses a username and password to login to the mail system.  
        If a client API key is required for login, then see the action ms_login_ClientSecret.<br/><br/>
        
				To use the authentication service provided by Azure Active Directory to enable Microsoft Graph, you will need to:<br/><br/>
        
				1) Register the application with Azure Active Directory.<br/>
				2) For Authentication, under "Advanced settings", set "Yes" to "Allow public client flows."<br/>
				3) For API permissions, under "Request API permissions", click "Microsoft Graph", choose "Delegated", add "User.Read" and "Mail.Send", and Grant admin consent.
				<br/><br/>
				<e>
					<b>ms_login("00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000", "aaaaaaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa", "user@organization.com", "@APPVAR(values/adv/password)")</b><br/>
					ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com","ccuser@organization.com","bccuser@organization.com","Document Integrity","Document Page Types and counts are accurate. Thanks for your help.")<br/>
					ms_SendMail()<br/>
					ms_logout()<br/><br/>

					If more than one logins (ms_login or ms_login_ClientSecret) are used, second action will be ignored unless authentication fails/expires or ms_logout() was used before the second action to disconnect.
				</e>
			</h>
			<lvl>All levels.</lvl>
			<ret>
				<b>True,</b> if the login succeeds.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
			</ret>
      <see>
        ms_login_ClientSecret to login using an API key.<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help topic in the RuleRunnerLogic action library for the complete smart parameter reference.<br/>
      </see>
		</method>

		<method name="ms_login_ClientSecret" qi="Login to Office 365 with a Client Secret using the Microsoft Graph API.">
			<p name="clientId" type="string" />
			<p name="tenantId" type="string" />
			<p name="clientSecret" type="string" />
			<p name="impersonatedUserId" type="string" />
			<ap>
				clientId : Application (client) ID from Azure Active Directory - App registrations.<br/>
				tenantId : Directory (tenant) ID from Azure Active Directory - App registrations.<br/>
				clientSecret : Client Secret from Azure Active Directory - App registrations.<br/>
				impersonatedUserId : User ID of Office 365 account to impersonate.<br/><br/>

				Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>

				Parameters (clientId, tenantId, clientSecret, impersonatedUserId) can be encrypted by storing it in the Advanced section of the Custom tab in the Application Manager.<br/><br/>
        
				To retrieve parameters that is stored in the Advanced section,
				use a smart parameter for the action's parameters similar to
				@APPVAR(values/adv/clientSecret) where clientSecret is the name entered for the value in the Application Manager.
			</ap>
			<h>
        This action will login to the Office 365 mail service, allowing emails to be sent from Datacap at runtime.
        Once logged in, the other actions in this email library can be used to create an email to send.
        The action uses a "client secret" API key to login to the mail system. Alternatively,
        the action ms_login can be used to login to the mail service using a username and password.<br/><br/>
        
				To use the authentication service provided by Azure Active Directory to enable Microsoft Graph, you will need to:<br/>
				1) Register the application with Azure Active Directory.<br/>
				2) For Certificates &amp; secrets, under "Client secrets", click "New client secret" to "Add" and get "Value."<br/>
				3) For API permissions, under "Request API permissions", click "Microsoft Graph", choose "Application", add "User.Read.All" and "Mail.Send", and Grant admin consent.
				<br/><br/>
				<e>
					<b>ms_login_ClientSecret("00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000", "aaaaaaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa", "@APPVAR(values/adv/clientSecret)", "user@organization.com")</b><br/>
					ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser@organization.com", "bccuser@organization.com","Document Integrity", "Document Page Types and counts are accurate. Thanks for your help.")<br/>
					ms_SendMail()<br/>
					ms_logout()<br/><br/>

					If more than one logins (ms_login or ms_login_ClientSecret) are used, second action will be ignored unless authentication fails/expires or ex_logout() was used before the second action to disconnect.
				</e>
			</h>
			<lvl>All levels.</lvl>
			<ret>
				<b>True,</b> if the login succeeds.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
			</ret>
      <see>
        ms_login to login using a username and password.<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help topic in the RuleRunnerLogic action library for the complete smart parameter reference.<br/>
      </see>
		</method>

		<method name="ms_SetMessage" qi="Set Message using the Microsoft Graph API.">
			<p name="to" type="string" />
			<p name="cc" type="string" />
			<p name="bcc" type="string" />
			<p name="subject" type="string" />
			<p name="body" type="string" />
			<ap>
				to : The To: recipients for the message.<br/>
				cc : The Cc: recipients for the message.<br/>
				bcc : The Bcc: recipients for the message.<br/>
				subject : The subject of the message.<br/>
				body : The body of the message.<br/><br/>

        Multiple email recipients can be specified by separating email addresses with a comma.
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
			<h>
        This action creates email message to be sent at runtime. The recipients, subject and body are created for the email using the action's parameters.
        <br/><br/>
				<e>
					<b>ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser@organization.com", "bccuser@organization.com", "Document Integrity", "Document Page Types and counts are accurate. Thanks for your help.")</b><br/>
					ms_SendMail()<br/><br/>

          The above example creates an email using static text for the recipients, subject and email body.<br/><br/>          

          <b>ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser1@organization.com,ccuser2@organization.com", "", "@P\SubjectField", "This was run in batch +@BATCHID+ and the page total is: +@P\TotalField")</b><br/>
          ms_SetAttachment("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)+\+@ID+.tif")<br/>
          ms_SendMail()<br/><br/>
          
          In this example, it is assume that the actions are running on a page level object.  The email's subject is created using the text contents of the field "SubjectField", off of the current page.
          The email subject is created using a mix of static and dynamic text. The current batch ID is placed into the email as well as the
          text value from the field called "TotalField".  The TIF for the current page is attached and then the email is sent.<br/><br/>

				</e>
			</h>
			<lvl>All levels.</lvl>
			<ret>
				<b>False</b> if the mail object cannot be initialized.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
			</ret>
      <see>
        ms_SetAttachment.
      </see>
		</method>

		<method name="ms_SetAttachment" qi="Set Attachment using the Microsoft Graph API.">
			<p name="path" type="string" />
			<ap>
				path : The file’s path, name and extension.<br/><br/>

				Smart parameters are supported.
			</ap>
			<h>
				Attaches the specified file to the current email created by the action ms_SetMessage.<br/><br/>

				ms_SetAttachment can be called multiple times to attach multiple files to an email before the action ms_SendMail. 
        The Microsoft Graph mail API limits the total number of attachments that can be added to less than 3 MB.<br/><br/>
				<e>
					ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser@organization.com", "bccuser@organization.com", "Document Integrity", "Document Page Types and counts are accurate. Thanks for your help.")<br/>
					<b>ms_SetAttachment("h:+\+MyDir+\+MQSW+\+export+\+@BATCHID+.txt")</b><br/>
					ms_SendMail()<br/><br/>

					This example attaches the Export file of the current batch to the email.  It looks for a text file named using the current batch ID.
          It would be expected that some previous step has created this text file using the current batch ID.<br/><br/>

          <b>ms_SetAttachment("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)+\+@ID+.txt")</b><br/><br/>

          In this example, it is assumed that the action is called on a page object and it will attach a text file that is associated
          with the current page. It is assume a previous step created a text file for the page.<br/><br/>
				</e>
			</h>
			<lvl>All levels.</lvl>
			<ret>
				<b>False</b> if the file does not exist or cannot be attached. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
			</ret>
      <see>
        ms_SetMessage.
      </see>
		</method>

		<method name="ms_SendMail" qi="Send Mail using the Microsoft Graph API.">
			<ap>None.</ap>
			<h>
				Sends an email assembled by previous actions from this action library using ms_SetMessage and ms_SetAttachment.
        Once an email is complete, use ms_SendMail to send the new message. After ms_SendMail
        is called, another email can be generated using ms_SetMessage, if necessary.<br/><br/>

				After sending, this action will discard the contents of the email in memory.  Calls to the email actions
				after ms_SendMail() will cause the creation of a new email message.
				<br/><br/>
				<e>
					ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser@organization.com", "bccuser@organization.com", "Document Integrity", "Document Page Types and counts are accurate. Thanks for your help.")<br/>
					<b>ms_SendMail()</b><br/><br/>
          
          The above example shows the combination of actions to create an email and send it. If multiple emails
          are to be created, ms_SendMail must be called to completed the email before calling ms_SetMessage again.<br/><br/>

          ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser@organization.com", "", "Email for batch: +@BATCHID","Here is the text file for page +@ID")<br/>
          ms_SetAttachment("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)+\+@ID+.txt")<br/>
          <b>ms_SendMail()</b><br/><br/>
          
          This example is similar to the first example, however it adjusts the text in the email subject and body based on runtime data. One file is attached
          and then the email is sent.<br/><br/>
				</e>
			</h>
			<lvl>All levels.</lvl>
			<ret>
				<b>False</b> if the rule does not include a previous ms_SetMessage() action, or if the email cannot be sent. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
			</ret>
      <see>
        ms_login, ms_login_ClientSecret and ms_SetMessage.
      </see>
		</method>

		<method name="ms_logout" qi="Disconnect from the Office 365 mail service.">
			<ap>None.</ap>
			<h>
				Closes the connection to the Office 365 mail service.
				Call once for each batch after ms_login and ms_login_ClientSecret() have completed.
				<br/><br/>
				<e>
          Send Mail Ruleset<br/>
          - Batch Level Open Rule<br/>
          - - Function<br/>
          - - ms_login_ClientSecret("00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000", "aaaaaaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa", "@APPVAR(values/adv/clientSecret)", "user@organization.com")<br/><br/>
          
          - Page Level Rule<br/>
          - - ms_SetMessage("touser@organization.com", "ccuser@organization.com", "", "Email for batch: +@BATCHID","Here is the text file for page +@ID")<br/>
          - - ms_SetAttachment("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)+\+@ID+.txt")<br/>
          - - ms_SendMail()<br/><br/>
          
          - Batch Level Close Rule<br/>
          - - <b>ms_logout()</b><br/><br/>
          
          This example shows how to setup a ruleset that will send emails on multiple pages within the batch.
          The first ruleset performs the login, which would typically occur only once for the batch.
          Next the page level ruleset will be called for pages that use it. For example, if the application has
          "Main" and "Trailing" pages, then this rule could be attached to just the "Main" pages, or just specific
          page types.  Then the page rule will be called for each of the pages that are attached to the rule, potentially
          generating multiple emails.  At the end of the batch level close event, the email logout is called.<br/>          
					<br/>
				</e>
			</h>
			<lvl>All levels.</lvl>
			<ret>
				Always <b>True.</b>
			</ret>
      <see>
        ms_login, ms_login_ClientSecret.
      </see>
		</method>

	</com>
</rrx>
